Decepticon
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Project:   Decepticon — an autonomous red-team agent framework
Source:    https://github.com/vitalii-honchar/decepticon
License:   Apache License, Version 2.0
           https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

Used by:   backend/state/governance.py
           backend/state/killchain.py
           backend/state/references/killchain.yaml
           backend/governance_draft.py

Derived from the following Decepticon source:

  tools/opplan.py
      The OPPLAN data model — Objective, OPPLAN, ObjectivePhase, ObjectiveStatus,
      C2Tier, OpsecLevel — and the objective status STATE MACHINE
      (pending -> in-progress -> completed / blocked / cancelled, with completed
      and cancelled terminal), plus the versioned-payload builder and summary.

  tools/defense/conops.py
      The Concept of Operations document structure and generator shape.

  middleware/roe.py
      The Rules of Engagement structure and the way an RoE frames authorized
      actions.

  tools/references/killchain.yaml
      The MITRE ATT&CK kill-chain reference used to map objectives to techniques
      and to render the coverage view.

Adaptation notes:
  * The data model and state machine were reconstructed from the above and
    reshaped onto HackPit's persistence: a shared, upsert-only SQLite store keyed
    by engagement session_id, rather than Decepticon's versioned JSON files.
  * The RoE is treated as a written frame the human approves against — advisory,
    NOT a machine veto. HackPit's standing model keeps per-command human approval
    as the actual bound; governance executes nothing and adds no gate.
  * Generation is propose-only: the drafter (backend/governance_draft.py) drafts
    the four documents from the scope + target for a human to edit and approve; it
    executes nothing an engagement acts on.

